Hypothesis: {"feature_idea":"REL_HIGH52: compute the relative price R_t = close_t / spy_t each week; take its rolling 52-week maximum; feature = log(R_t / max_{52w} R). Then cs_z across the 9 sectors. In the DSL: {\"name\":\"REL_HIGH52\",\"expr\":{\"op\":\"cs_z\",\"x\":{\"op\":\"log\",\"x\":{\"op\":\"ratio\",\"a\":{\"op\":\"ratio\",\"a\":{\"input\":\"close\"},\"b\":{\"input\":\"spy\"}},\"b\":{\"op\":\"rolling\",\"stat\":\"max\",\"n\":52,\"x\":{\"op\":\"ratio\",\"a\":{\"input\":\"close\"},\"b\":{\"input\":\"spy\"}}}}}}} (10 nodes). Values are <= 0; sectors at a new relative high score 0 and get the top z-score. Optional variant if overlap with REV1 is a concern: replace the numerator with lag n=1 of the relative price so the last week's move is excluded.","mechanism":"Anchoring on the 52-week high (George & Hwang 2004): investors use the one-year high as a reference point and are reluctant to bid a sector above it even when fundamentals justify it, so good news is impounded slowly and sectors trading near their 52-week relative high keep outperforming over the following weeks, while sectors far below their high (where the anchor is stale and bad news is also under-reacted to) keep lagging. Measuring the high on the sector-to-SPY relative price strips out the common market swing, so the anchor is about sector-specific standing rather than whether the whole market made a new high. Expected sign: positive (closer to the 52-week relative high -> higher relative return over the next 4 weeks).","rationale":"DD13 is a drawdown from a 13-week high on the absolute close, so it is dominated by the last quarter's market move and is strongly tied to REV1 and MOM26_1 (corr 0.40 and 0.49); a one-year relative-price anchor is a different horizon and a level (position inside the past year's range) rather than a return. MOM26_1 and MOM52_4 are point-to-point returns, which can be identical for a sector that climbed steadily to a fresh high and one that spiked and then collapsed; the nearness-to-high term distinguishes these paths. Because it is built on close/SPY it is also roughly beta-neutral, unlike VOL13/BETA52 which are near-collinear (0.96) and would otherwise absorb any market-driven high/low effect."}
