{"mechanism": "Long-horizon relative reversal. Sectors that strongly outperformed the market one to two years ago attract capital, capacity expansion and analyst over-extrapolation; as supply responds and valuations normalise, their relative returns over the following months tend to lag (DeBondt–Thaler long-term reversal, sector capital-cycle effects). Conversely sectors that lagged the index in year -2 have had time for capacity to shrink and expectations to reset, so their relative returns over the next 4 weeks should be modestly positive. The effect is slow-moving, which suits the score smoothing and low turnover of the book, and it is a different horizon regime from the 1-week and 6-12 month windows the base model already sees.",
"rationale": "All six base features look back at most 52 weeks (MOM52_4 covers roughly t-52 to t-4, MOM26_1 t-26 to t-1, DD13/VOL13 13 weeks, REV1 one week, BETA52 a covariance not a return). No feature measures relative performance in the window t-104 to t-52, so year-2 reversal is unspanned; it should be only mildly (negatively) correlated with the momentum features because its window does not overlap theirs. It also differs from the earlier candidates: REL_HIGH52 (distance from 52w relative high), IVOL_SHOCK (volatility ratio) and REL_IR26 (risk-adjusted 26w momentum) are all within-52-week signals.",
"feature_idea": "Compute the relative log price r(t) = log(close(t)/spy(t)). Take the 52-week relative return ending 52 weeks ago: x(t) = r(t-52) - r(t-104), i.e. diff with n=52 applied to lag n=52 of log(ratio(close, spy)). Cross-sectionally z-score it each Friday: cs_z(diff(52, lag(52, log(ratio(close,spy))))). The model is expected to assign a negative weight (high year-2 relative return -> lower expected relative return). Name: REL_LTREV_104_52. Optionally a mul_state with BEAR24 could be tested later, since reversal is often stronger after market drawdowns, but the plain cs_z version is the proposal."}

